          "question": "I get chest discomfort only when I am anxious, never on exertion. How do I tell the difference, and what does guidance describe about assessing it?",
          "answer": "You should seek medical advice promptly if your chest discomfort changes in intensity, frequency, or if new symptoms arise.\n\nThe chest discomfort you've described occurring only during anxiety is relevant for assessment. Clinical guidelines indicate that it's vital to differentiate between anxiety-related symptoms and potential cardiac issues (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention). Common causes of chest discomfort in anxious patients include panic attacks, which can mimic cardiac symptoms, as well as conditions like coronary artery disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) (condition-gerd, condition-coronary-artery-disease).\n\nWhen assessing chest discomfort, an emergency evaluation is crucial, especially if the discomfort is associated with other symptoms such as shortness of breath, nausea, or dizziness (Emergency medicine and cardiology society guidance). It is stressed that clinicians should not assume the discomfort is solely due to anxiety without ruling out cardiac causes, particularly in patients with risk factors (General clinical reference).\n\nDiffering guidance exists regarding the immediate evaluation of chest discomfort linked to anxiety versus potential organic causes. Some guidelines advocate an emergency evaluation to exclude serious conditions, while others may manage anxiety without immediate cardiac assessment if the symptoms indicate a low risk (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention). Regular follow-up may be advised for those with anxiety-related chest discomfort, especially if symptoms evolve or worsen (Guideline on coronary artery disease secondary prevention).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
